                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A good facsimile of Lords of Chaos. The maps so far are significantly smaller than Lords of Chaos maps, and don't appear to have the random dispersement of goods, which may be a positive or a negative difference - it kind of loses the \"gamble\" effect that Lords of Chaos had where you could spend your points on dragon summon spells and then not be able to find the ingredients to cast those spells. To some extent that was fun, but it was also annoying, so unknown overall.\n\nThere's some awkward control issues, which I think are from the decision to make click detection \"what is in front of the pointer\" rather than \"what is on the square that's in front of the pointer\", which in 3D often makes it difficult to e.g. summon something on the square \"behind\" your warlock, you have to spin the camera to make that square accessible, which adds friction. But it's not *as* awkward as the original with its early 90s UI.\n\nAlso doesn't crash as much as Lords of Chaos did, so that's a positive.\n\nOverall it's not as strong of a recommendation as I would expect from what I've said, because it turns out my love of Lords of Chaos might be significantly nostalgia - turns out turn-based \"magic\" tactics isn't really as fun as I remember. I would recommend \"I Am Not A Monster: First Contact\" over this, but if you've already played that, this is good too. :)",
